tel 1.0

base kaleidoscope
import soundness

info   regular expressions and globs as pattern extractors, checked and typed as the code compiles
detail
    _Kaleidoscope_ writes a regular expression as `r"…"` and a glob as `g"…"`, and makes each
    a **pattern**: in a `match`, `case r"hello $name(.*)"` tests the text and binds `name` to
    what the group matched. The expression is parsed while the code compiles, so an unbalanced
    bracket, a `{2,1}` repetition or a capture nowhere a group follows is a compile error at
    the offending character, and each `$name` is typed from the pattern it stands before: a
    plain group binds a `Text`, a single character class or shorthand (`[aeiou]`, `\d`, `.`)
    binds a `Char`, a repeated group (`+`, `*`, `{n,m}`) binds a `List`, and an optional group
    (`?`) binds an `Optional`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"hello world" match
      case r"$first(hello) $second(world)" => List(first, second)   // List(t"hello", t"world")
      case _                               => Nil

    t"foo123bar" match
      case r"foo$digits\d+bar" => digits   // List('1', '2', '3')
      case _                   => Nil
    ```

    Outside a pattern the same interpolators are expressions whose value is a `Regex`: its
    source `pattern`, the position and nature of every `Regex.Group`, and the matching methods
    `matches`, `seek` and `search`. `Regex` is the type Soundness uses wherever a regular
    expression is meant, and `Text` is never used for one, so a signature such as gossamer's
    `sub(from: Regex, to: Text)` says which of its arguments is a pattern. A `Glob` is the
    wildcard syntax of file paths — `*` within a segment, `**` across segments, `?` for one
    character, `[a-z]` and `[!a-z]` — translated to a regular expression.

    A regex is tagged with its **engine** as the `Form` of a `Regex in JavaBaseRegex` or a
    `Regex in Re2`, and the `Regex.Engine` typeclass for that form does the matching. The
    default is `JavaBaseRegex`, the platform's `java.util.regex`, whose backtracking accepts
    every construct of its syntax but can take exponential time on a pathological pattern and
    input. `Re2` is praxinoscope's automaton engine, which matches in time linear in the input
    and rejects, at compile time for a literal, the constructs an automaton cannot express
    (backreferences, lookaround, possessive quantifiers, atomic groups). The engine of a literal
    is chosen by importing one `RegexBackend` given from the `regexBackends` package by name,
    since `import soundness.*` imports no givens; a `Regex` built at runtime is retagged with
    `Regex#re2`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import regexBackends.re2

    val greeting: Regex in Re2 = r"hello (.*)"
    greeting.matches(t"hello world")   // true, in time linear in the input

    r"a*".subsumes(r"aa")              // true: every input `aa` matches, `a*` matches too
    ```

    The library is a single source set, `kaleidoscope.core`, which compiles for the JVM,
    Scala.js and Scala Native, taking `java.util.regex` or the platform's emulation of it. A
    stateful match — gossamer's `extract`, which walks through successive matches of a text —
    drives the extractors through a `Scanner`, a mutable cursor held exclusively. Everything is
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and `regexBackends` merges with the umbrella's own
    package of that name, so a program writes `import soundness.*` and then, if it wants the
    linear-time engine, `import regexBackends.re2`.

refer kaleidoscope.Regex
refer kaleidoscope.Glob
refer kaleidoscope.r
refer kaleidoscope.g
refer kaleidoscope.regexBackends
refer praxinoscope.Motif

entry .r   the `r"…"` interpolator: a regular expression as a pattern, or as a `Regex` value
    `r"…"` is a transparent inline extension on `StringContext` which parses the regular
    expression as the code compiles. In a `case`, it is an extractor: the pattern must match
    the whole text (through the `Scanner` given in scope; see `Scanner`), and each
    `$name` binds the capturing group written immediately after it. In expression position it
    is a `Regex in JavaBaseRegex`, or a `Regex in Re2` when `regexBackends.re2` is imported.

    A `$name` must stand directly before a group, a character class, `.` or one of the
    shorthands `\d`, `\D`, `\w`, `\W`, `\s`, `\S`; the type of the binding follows from what
    it precedes and the quantifier after it.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A literal pattern, matched or not
      code
          t"foo5bar" match
            case r"foo\dbar" => true
            case _           => false
          // true
    frame
      label A shorthand bound to `digit`: a `Char`
      code
          t"foo5bar" match
            case r"foo$digit\dbar" => digit
            case _                 => ' '
          // '5'
    frame
      label Repeated with `+`: a `List[Char]`
      code
          t"foo123bar" match
            case r"foo$digits\d+bar" => digits
            case _                   => Nil
          // List('1', '2', '3')
    frame
      label Made optional with `?`: an `Optional[Char]`
      code
          t"foobar" match
            case r"foo$digit\d?bar" => digit
            case _                  => Unset
          // Unset
    frame
      label A second group, in parentheses: a `Text`, and the two bind as a pair
      code
          t"foo123bar" match
            case r"foo$digits\d+$word(bar)" => (digits, word)
            case _                          => (Nil, t"")
          // (List('1', '2', '3'), t"bar")
    ##
    ```

    A group with no `$name` is matched but not bound, so `r"(hello) $second(world)"` requires
    the first word and binds only the second. Groups nest, as `r"(($first(hello)) world)"`
    does. A capture inside a repeating group is forbidden (`Regex.Error.Reason.Uncapturable`),
    since it cannot bind a fixed set of variables. On the left of a `val`, a pattern asserts
    that the extraction succeeds and binds all its captures at once:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val r"$prefix([a-z0-9._%+-]+)@$domain([a-z0-9.-]+)\.$tld([a-z]{2,6})" =
      t"test@example.com": @unchecked

    List(prefix, domain, tld)   // List(t"test", t"example", t"com")
    ```

    Inside `r"…"` a backslash is a backslash — only the regular expression's own escaping
    applies, so a literal parenthesis is `r"\("` — and `$` is written `$$` to mean itself. A
    pattern which does not parse is a compile error naming the reason and pointing at the
    offending character, _[↯SN-397.1] the regular expression could not be parsed because a
    capturing group was not closed at 11_; a `$name` with no group after it is
    _[↯SN-397.2] … a capturing group was expected immediately following an extractor_. Under
    `regexBackends.re2` the pattern is also checked against the RE2 subset, so a
    backreference or a lookaround is rejected at compile time rather than at its first use.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"" match { case r"hello(world" => () }   // does not compile
    ```

    A `Regex` value used in a pattern (`case ipAddress() =>`) matches as a whole and binds no
    groups; captures are available only where the pattern is written literally, since that is
    where the compiler can see them.

entry .g   the `g"…"` interpolator: a glob as a pattern, or as a `Regex` value
    `g"…"` writes a glob — the wildcard syntax of file paths — and is otherwise `r"…"`: a
    pattern in a `case`, a `Regex` in expression position, and compiled under the engine
    `regexBackends` selects. `*` matches any run of characters other than `/` and `\`, `**`
    matches across segments, `?` matches exactly one character other than a separator, `[a-z]`
    is a range and `[!a-z]` its negation, and every other character is itself. A `$name` binds
    one path segment (anything up to the next separator), so it needs no group after it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"/home/work/docs" match
      case g"/$home/$work/docs" => (home, work)   // (t"home", t"work")
      case _                    => (t"", t"")

    t"/home/work/docs" match
      case g"/$home/**" => home   // t"home"
      case _            => t""

    t"file.txt" match
      case g"*.txt" => true
      case _        => false
    ```

    A glob and a regular expression which translate to the same pattern are equal —
    `g".local/*" == r"\.local/[^/\\]*"` — and `Glob.parse(text).regex` shows the translation.
    A glob is total: every text is a glob, so `g"…"` never fails to compile.

entry .RegexBackend   the marker a `regexBackends` given carries, read by `r"…"` and `g"…"` as they expand
    `RegexBackend[form]` is a sealed phantom trait with no members. The `r"…"` and `g"…"`
    macros summon a `RegexBackend[Re2]` while they expand: when `regexBackends.re2` is
    imported the literal is compiled for praxinoscope's engine, checked against the RE2
    subset, and tagged `Regex in Re2`; otherwise it is tagged `Regex in JavaBaseRegex`.
    `Glob#matches` takes the same given, defaulting to `regexBackends.javaBaseRegex`. Nothing
    else reads it, and a program never implements it.

entry .regexBackends   the two matching engines, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which engine a literal pattern compiles for is decided while the code compiles, by the
      `RegexBackend` given in scope, and `import soundness.*` brings neither in: the default,
      with no import, is `java.util.regex`, and the linear-time engine is chosen by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import regexBackends.re2

      t"hello world" match
        case r"hello $name(.*)" => name   // t"world", in time linear in the input
        case _                  => t""
      ```

      The two engines agree on which inputs match, where a match begins and ends, and what
      each capture group binds, for every pattern both accept. They differ in what they
      accept: `java.util.regex` takes backreferences, lookaround, possessive quantifiers and
      atomic groups, and may take exponential time on a pathological pattern; RE2 rejects
      those constructs at compile time and is linear in the input, and additionally offers
      the `subsumes` and `intersects` analyses on a `Regex in Re2`. The import is scoped like
      any other, so one block of a file can use RE2 while the rest keeps the default.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Under the default engine, `java.util.regex`
        code
            val regex = r"(a|b)*c"
            // regex: Regex in JavaBaseRegex
            regex.matches(t"ababc")   // true
      frame
        label The same literal under RE2: the same answer, and a different type
        code
            locally:
              import regexBackends.re2
              val regex = r"(a|b)*c"
              // regex: Regex in Re2
              regex.matches(t"ababc")   // true
      frame
        label A construct RE2 cannot express is a compile error, not a runtime surprise
        code
            locally:
              import regexBackends.re2
              r"(a)\1"   // does not compile: backreferences are not part of RE2 syntax
      ##
      ```
  entry .javaBaseRegex   the platform's `java.util.regex` engine: the default, so the import is rarely needed
      `RegexBackend[JavaBaseRegex]`, the engine a literal compiles for when nothing is imported.
      It exists so that the default can be named — in a scope which has imported `re2` and
      wants one literal under the backtracking engine, say — and as `Glob#matches`'s default.
  entry .re2   praxinoscope's RE2 engine: linear-time matching, the RE2 subset, checked as the code compiles
      `RegexBackend[Re2]`. With it imported, every `r"…"` and `g"…"` in scope is parsed into
      its praxinoscope `Motif` while the code compiles, rejected there if it uses a
      backreference, lookaround, a possessive quantifier or an atomic group, and staged into
      the extractor so that no parsing or compilation happens at runtime. A literal with no
      captures is further compiled to a static finite automaton (`Fsa`), an allocation-free
      whole-input test, when its state count stays within the generator's budget. A literal in
      expression position is a `Regex in Re2`, on which `subsumes` and `intersects` are
      available.

entry .Regex   a parsed regular expression: its pattern, its groups, and matching through its engine
  detail
      A `Regex` is the source `#pattern` as `Text` and the tree of its `#groups`, each a
      `Regex.Group` recording the offsets of a parenthesised group, a character class or a
      single-character token, its quantifier and greed, and whether it is captured by a
      `$name`. It is the value of an `r"…"` or `g"…"` in expression position, and is built at
      runtime from text with `.apply` or `.parse`, which raise `Regex.Error` at the position
      and for the reason a pattern cannot be parsed.

      A regex is `Formal`: its `Form` type member names the engine which matches it, and the
      operations `#matches`, `#seek` and `#search` take a `Form is Regex.Engine` which resolves
      from that tag. A `Regex in JavaBaseRegex` — the type of `.apply`, `.parse` and every
      literal compiled without `regexBackends.re2` — dispatches to `java.util.regex`; a
      `Regex in Re2` dispatches to praxinoscope, and is made from a literal under
      `import regexBackends.re2` or from any regex with `#re2`, which checks the pattern
      against the RE2 subset as it retags. A bare `Regex` has no engine and cannot match.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val regex: Regex in JavaBaseRegex = Regex(t"[a-z]+@[a-z]+\\.com")
      regex.matches(t"jon@example.com")          // true
      regex.seek(t"mail jon@example.com now")    // the interval of the match
      regex.search(t"a@b.com, c@d.com")          // a chain of two intervals

      val linear: Regex in Re2 = regex.re2
      linear.matches(t"jon@example.com")         // true, in linear time
      ```

      The matching operations are methods rather than extensions so that they can never be
      shadowed by the generic collection extensions of the same names, `seek` and `search`,
      which the `soundness` package also exports. Two regexes are equal when their patterns
      and group trees are, so `g".local/*" == r"\.local/[^/\\]*"` holds, but `r"[xy]"` and
      `r"[yx]"` are unequal though they match the same inputs.

      `Regex` is used throughout Soundness wherever a pattern is meant: gossamer's `sub`,
      `cut` and `search` on `Text` take one, and jacinta's JSON blueprints type their `regex`
      accessors as `Regex in JavaBaseRegex`.
  refer kaleidoscope.r
  refer kaleidoscope.regexBackends
  refer kaleidoscope.JavaBaseRegex
  refer praxinoscope.Re2
  refer praxinoscope.Motif
  entry .apply   parses one text as a regular expression for the `java.util.regex` engine
      `Regex(text)` is `Regex.parse(List(text))`: a `Regex in JavaBaseRegex` with no captured
      groups, or `Regex.Error` raised through a `Tactic[Regex.Error]` when the text does not
      parse. For a pattern known as the code is written, `r"…"` checks it at compile time
      instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Regex(t"ab{3,1}c")   // raises Regex.Error(5, BadRepetition)
      ```
  entry .parse   parses the parts of an interpolated pattern, each boundary marking a captured group
      `Regex.parse(parts)` is the parser behind `r"…"`, available at runtime. The parts are the
      pieces of the pattern between its `$name` substitutions, so each part after the first
      must begin with a group, a character class, `.` or a `\d`-style shorthand
      (`Regex.Error.Reason.ExpectedGroup` otherwise), and that element is marked as captured.
      The result is a `Regex in JavaBaseRegex` and needs a `Tactic[Regex.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Regex.parse(List(t"aa", t"(bb)cc"))
      // Regex(t"aa(bb)cc", List(Regex.Group(3, 5, 6, Nil, Exactly(1), Greedy, true)))
      ```

      The parser rejects an unclosed group, a stray `)`, an empty character class `[]`, a
      malformed `{n,m}` repetition, a maximum below the minimum or equal to zero, and a
      captured group inside a repeating group (`Uncapturable`), which cannot bind a fixed set
      of variables. It then checks the whole with `java.util.regex.Pattern`, reporting any
      further rejection as `InvalidPattern`.
  entry .unsafeFrom   builds a regex from parts already known to parse, with no error channel
      `Regex.unsafeFrom(parts)(using Unsafe)` is `parse` with its `Regex.Error` discarded, for
      parts a macro has already validated: the `r"…"` extractors call it on every match. The
      erased `Unsafe` token marks that the caller, not the type system, asserts the parts are
      valid; a part which does not parse throws.
  entry .makePattern   renders a group tree back to pattern text, naming or plainly parenthesising its groups
      The renderer behind `#capturePattern`: walks `todo` (the groups of `pattern` from offset
      `last` to `end`), wrapping each in parentheses — named `(?<gN>…)` with `index` counting
      up when `named` is true, plain `(…)` when it is false — and a quantified group in a
      second inner pair. Returns the next group index and the rendered text. It is public for
      the engines' use; a program reads `#capturePattern` instead.
  entry .Engine   the typeclass which matches a `Regex` of a given `Form`: `java.util.regex` or RE2
    detail
        `form is Regex.Engine` is how a `Regex in form` matches. The instance for
        `JavaBaseRegex` is `JavaBaseRegex.engine`, in that type's companion; the instance for
        `Re2` is `.re2`, in this companion, since praxinoscope's `Re2` sits below this library
        and cannot name `Regex`. Both are in implicit scope, so a `Regex in Re2` never falls
        back to `java.util.regex` and neither needs an import. Each engine implements whole-
        input and scanner-driven `#matches`, leftmost `#seek` and repeated `#search`, and the
        capture-group extraction the `r"…"` extractors use.

        An engine is addressed through the methods of `Regex` (`regex.matches(text)`), not
        called directly; the trait is public so that `Regex.Engine.re2` and
        `JavaBaseRegex.engine` can be named.
    entry .re2   the engine instance for `Regex in Re2`, bridging to praxinoscope's `Motif`
        `Re2 is Regex.Engine`. It looks up the praxinoscope `Motif` of the regex by its
        pattern, re-rendered with a plain parenthesis around every group, in a cache every
        `Regex in Re2` was admitted to when it was made — by the `r"…"` expansion under
        `regexBackends.re2`, which stages the motif into the extractor, or by `Regex#re2`,
        which parses it then. A `Regex in Re2` which was never admitted is a defect and panics
        rather than raising. Capture groups are mapped onto praxinoscope's paren-ordered group
        numbers; a repeating group's iterations are recovered by rescanning its span with the
        group's body. Non-overlapping `search` restarts one past the end of each match, as the
        `JavaBaseRegex` engine does, so immediately adjacent matches are skipped alike.
    entry #Self   the form this engine matches: `JavaBaseRegex` or `Re2`
    entry #matches   whether a regex matches the whole text, or the next match from a `Scanner`'s position
        With the `Scanner` given's `nextStart` unset, a whole-input match. With it set to a
        position, the next match found at or after it, recording its start plus one in
        `nextStart` and its end in `matchEnd`, which is how gossamer's `extract` walks a text.
    entry #seek   the interval of the leftmost match at or after `start`, or `Unset`
    entry #search   every match from `start` as a lazy `Chain` of intervals, overlapping or not
        With `overlap` false, each search resumes one past the previous match's end; with it
        true, one past the previous match's start, so matches may share text.
  entry .Greed   how a quantified group competes for input: greedy, reluctant or possessive
    detail
        The suffix after a quantifier: none for `Greedy` (as much as possible), `?` for
        `Reluctant` (as little as possible), `+` for `Possessive` (as much as possible, never
        giving any back). `Regex.Group#greed` records it, and `#serialize` renders it. The RE2
        engine rejects a possessive quantifier, which an automaton cannot express.
    entry #serialize   the suffix: empty, `?` or `+`
  entry .Quantifier   how many times a group repeats: exactly, at least, or between two counts
    detail
        `Exactly(n)` is `{n}` (and `Exactly(1)` an unquantified group), `AtLeast(n)` is
        `{n,}` (so `AtLeast(0)` is `*` and `AtLeast(1)` is `+`), and `Between(n, m)` is `{n,m}`
        (so `Between(0, 1)` is `?`). The quantifier decides a capture's type: `Exactly(1)`
        binds a `Text` or `Char`, `Between(0, 1)` an `Optional` of it, and anything else a
        `List`.
    entry #serialize   the quantifier as written: empty for `Exactly(1)`, `*`, `+`, `?` or a `{…}` form
    entry #unitary   whether this is `Exactly(1)`, the quantifier of an unrepeated group
  entry .Group   one group of a pattern: its offsets, its quantifier, and whether it is captured
    detail
        A `Group` records a parenthesised group, a character class or — when captured by a
        `$name` — a single-character token (`.`, `\d`, `\w`, …) by the offsets of its content
        in the pattern: `#start` and `#end` bound the content, `#outerEnd` is the offset after
        the closing bracket and any quantifier, and `#outerStart` the offset of the opening
        one. Nested groups are `#groups`. The `r"…"` macro reads the tree to type each capture
        and the engines read it to rebuild the pattern and to extract groups.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Regex.parse(List(t"aa(bb)+?cc")).groups
        // List(Regex.Group(3, 5, 8, Nil, AtLeast(1), Reluctant))
        ```
    entry #start   the offset of the group's first content character
    entry #end   the offset after the group's last content character
    entry #outerEnd   the offset after the group's closing bracket and quantifier
    entry #outerStart   the offset of the group's opening bracket, or of the token itself for a bare shorthand
    entry #groups   the groups nested directly inside this one
    entry #quantifier   how often the group repeats, `Exactly(1)` when unquantified
    entry #greed   whether the repetition is greedy, reluctant or possessive
    entry #capture   whether a `$name` captures this group
    entry #charClass   whether this group is a `[…]` character class rather than a parenthesised group
    entry #singleChar   whether this group is a single-character token: `.` or a `\d`-style shorthand
    entry #charMatcher   whether the group matches one character at a time: a class or a single-character token
        A char-matching group binds a `Char` (or a `List[Char]`, or `Optional[Char]`) where a
        parenthesised group binds `Text`.
    entry #allGroups   every group nested at any depth, in pattern order
    entry #captureGroups   every captured group nested at any depth, in pattern order
    entry #serialize   renders this group as pattern text, with a named or plain capturing parenthesis
        Given the whole pattern, the next capture index and whether to name the group
        `(?<gN>…)`, returns the index after this group and its text; a quantified
        parenthesised group gains an inner `(…)` so the name wraps the whole repetition. Used
        by `Regex.makePattern`.
  entry .Error   raised when a pattern cannot be parsed: the position and the reason
    detail
        Error 397: _the regular expression could not be parsed because `reason` at `index`_,
        with the reason's number as the error's sub-number, so an unclosed group is
        `SN-397.1`. Raised by `Regex.apply` and `Regex.parse` through a `Tactic[Regex.Error]`,
        and reported as a compile error, pointing at the offending character, by `r"…"`. The
        index counts characters of the parts joined together, with nothing standing in for
        the `$name` substitutions.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        safely(Regex(t"aa(bb){2,1}c"))   // Unset: Regex.Error(9, BadRepetition)
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a pattern was rejected, as one of eleven numbered cases
      detail
          `UnclosedGroup` (1), _a capturing group was not closed_; `ExpectedGroup` (2), _a
          capturing group was expected immediately following an extractor_, for a `$name`
          with no group after it; `BadRepetition` (3), _the maximum number of repetitions is
          less than the minimum_; `Uncapturable` (4), _a capturing group inside a repeating
          group can not be extracted_; `UnexpectedChar` (5), _the repetition range contained
          an unexpected character_; `NotInGroup` (6), _a closing parenthesis was found without
          a corresponding opening parenthesis_; `IncompleteRepetition` (7), _the repetition
          range was not closed_; `InvalidPattern` (8), _the pattern was invalid_, for anything
          `java.util.regex` rejects that the parser did not; `UnclosedEscape` (9), _nothing
          followed the escape character `\`_; `EmptyCharClass` (10), _the character class is
          empty_; and `ZeroMaximum` (11), _the maximum number of repetitions must be greater
          than zero_. Each case's `number` is the error's sub-number.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as the message quoted on `Regex.Error.Reason`
      entry #number   the reason's number, 1 to 11, which is the sub-number of its `Regex.Error`
    entry #index   the offset in the pattern at which the error was found
    entry #reason   the `Regex.Error.Reason`
  entry #pattern   the pattern as written, with its `$name`s removed
  entry #groups   the top-level groups of the pattern, each a `Regex.Group` with its own nested groups
  entry #re2   this regex retagged for the RE2 engine, checked now against the RE2 subset
      Parses the pattern into its praxinoscope `Motif` (raising `Motif.Error` through a
      `Tactic[Motif.Error]` for a backreference, lookaround, possessive quantifier, atomic
      group or anything else outside RE2 syntax), admits it to the engine's cache, and
      returns the same regex as a `Regex in Re2`. Every `Regex in Re2` is therefore a checked
      pattern, and its first match never fails for a syntax reason.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val linear: Regex in Re2 = Regex(t"(a|b)*c").re2
      linear.matches(t"ababc")   // true
      Regex(t"(a)\\1").re2       // raises Motif.Error: backreferences are not part of RE2 syntax
      ```
  entry #unapply   a `Regex` value as a pattern: `case regex() =>` matches the whole text
      Matches without a scanner, so the whole text must match, and binds nothing: a value's
      groups are not visible to the compiler, so only a literal `r"…"` can bind captures.
  entry #matches   whether the text matches, wholly or from the `Scanner`'s position
      Needs the `Form is Regex.Engine` for this regex's engine (in implicit scope for both
      forms) and a `Scanner^`, whose default given is a fresh scanner with no position, so a
      plain call is a whole-input match. A scanner with `nextStart` set finds the next match
      from that position and records where it was.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Regex(t"ab?c").matches(t"ac")    // true
      Regex(t"ab?c").matches(t"xacx")  // false: the whole text must match
      ```
  entry #seek   the interval of the leftmost match at or after `start`, or `Unset` if there is none
      `start` is an `Ordinal`, `Prim` by default. The `Interval` is zero-based over the
      text's UTF-16 units, as gossamer's `segment` takes it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Regex(t"o").seek(t"foo")   // Interval.zerary(1, 2)
      Regex(t"z").seek(t"foo")   // Unset
      ```
  entry #search   every match at or after `start`, as a lazily-built `Chain` of intervals
      With `overlap` false (the default) the search resumes one past the end of each match;
      with it true, one past the start, so a match may begin inside the previous one. The
      `Chain` is built as it is read, so a search over a long text costs only what is
      consumed. Gossamer's `text.search(regex)` maps the intervals to the matched segments.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Regex(t"a").search(t"banana")
      // Chain(Interval.zerary(1, 2), Interval.zerary(3, 4), Interval.zerary(5, 6))
      ```
  entry #capturePattern   the pattern rendered with every group parenthesised and each capture named `g0`, `g1`, …
      The text handed to `java.util.regex`: a captured group becomes `(?<gN>…)`, numbered in
      pattern order, and a quantified captured group is wrapped so the name covers the whole
      repetition, as `a(?<g0>(bc)*)` for `a$xs(bc)*`.
  entry #subsumes   whether this regex matches every input the other does; RE2 forms only
      `L(that) ⊆ L(this)`, decided by praxinoscope's `Subsumption` over the two compiled
      programs. Both regexes must be `Regex in Re2` — the question is decidable only without
      backreferences and lookaround — and the analysis raises `Motif.Error` for a pattern it
      cannot verify (one with a word or line boundary, `Unverifiable`) or a pair which
      exhausts its state budget (`BudgetExceeded`).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import regexBackends.re2
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      r"a*".subsumes(r"aa")         // true
      r"aa".subsumes(r"a*")         // false
      r"[a-z]+".subsumes(r"abc|def") // true
      ```
  entry #intersects   whether some input matches both this regex and the other; RE2 forms only
      Reachability, in the product of the two automata, of a point where both accept; a
      polynomial question, where `#subsumes` is worst-case exponential. Both regexes must be
      `Regex in Re2`, and a word or line boundary in either raises `Motif.Error`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import regexBackends.re2
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      r"[a-m]+".intersects(r"[k-z]+")   // true
      r"[a-m]+".intersects(r"[n-z]+")   // false
      ```
  entry #allGroups   every group at any depth, in pattern order
  entry #captureGroups   every captured group at any depth, in pattern order: the groups `r"…"` binds

entry .JavaBaseRegex   the `java.util.regex` engine's marker: the default `Form` of every `Regex`
  detail
      A sealed phantom trait with no members, naming the platform's `java.util.regex` (or,
      on Scala.js and Scala Native, the platform's emulation of it) as the engine of a
      `Regex in JavaBaseRegex`. It is the form of `Regex.apply`, `Regex.parse` and of every
      `r"…"` and `g"…"` compiled without `regexBackends.re2`. Its engine instance `.engine`
      lives here, in the subject's companion, so no import is needed.

      The engine compiles each distinct `Regex#capturePattern` once into a cached
      `java.util.regex.Pattern`, so matching many texts against one regex costs one
      compilation. Its backtracking accepts every construct of the Java syntax, including
      backreferences and lookaround, at the cost of exponential time on a pathological
      pattern and input; `regexBackends.re2` is the linear-time alternative.
  refer kaleidoscope.Regex
  refer kaleidoscope.regexBackends
  entry .engine   the `Regex.Engine` instance which matches a `Regex in JavaBaseRegex`
      `JavaBaseRegex is Regex.Engine`, in implicit scope. Whole-input `matches` is
      `Matcher#matches`; a scanner-driven match is `Matcher#find(position)`; `seek` and
      `search` are `find` from the start position, resuming one past each match's end (or
      start, with overlap). A repeating captured group's iterations are recovered by rematching
      the group's body over its span.

entry .Scanner   a mutable cursor through a text, which a sequence of matches advances
  detail
      A `Scanner` carries the position at which the next match should begin (`#nextStart`)
      and where the last match ended (`#matchEnd`). The `r"…"` and `g"…"` extractors and
      `Regex#matches` take one as `(using Scanner^)`: with `nextStart` unset, the match is a
      whole-input match, which is what a `case r"…"` in ordinary code does; with it set, the
      engine finds the next match at or after that position and advances the scanner to one
      past its start, which is how gossamer's `extract` steps through every match in a text.

      The class is `caps.Mutable`, since a match changes its state, so only an exclusive
      reference may drive it: a hand-written given is declared `given (Scanner^) =
      Scanner(Unset)`, and a bare `given Scanner = …` is read-only and is rejected where a
      match needs it. The default given `.default` makes a fresh positionless scanner at every
      use.
  refer kaleidoscope.Regex
  entry .default   a fresh scanner with no position, so an unqualified match is whole-input
      Typed `Scanner^` and built anew at each summon (through an erased `DummyImplicit`), so
      no two matches share a cursor unless a program binds one deliberately.
  entry #nextStart   the position the next match searches from, or `Unset` for a whole-input match
      After a scanner-driven match, one past the match's start, so a repeated match finds
      the next occurrence; a text with no further match leaves it unchanged.
  entry #matchEnd   where the last scanner-driven match ended, or `Unset` before any

entry .Glob   a glob — `*`, `**`, `?`, `[a-z]` wildcards — as tokens, translated to a regular expression
  detail
      A `Glob` is a sequence of `Glob.Token`s parsed from the wildcard syntax of file paths,
      and `#regex` is its translation to a regular expression: `*` becomes `[^/\\]*` (any run
      not crossing a separator), `**` becomes `.*`, `?` becomes `[^/\\]`, a range or set
      carries across with `!` as negation, and every other character is itself, escaped if the
      regular-expression syntax would read it otherwise. The `g"…"` interpolator is the glob as
      a pattern; `.parse` reads one from text at runtime, and `.decodable` lets a glob-valued
      flag or configuration value be read with `as[Glob]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Glob.parse(t"hello*world").regex        // t"hello[^/\\]*world"
      Glob.parse(t"hello[!a-z]world").regex   // t"hello[^a-z]world"
      Glob.parse(t"**/*.jar").matches(t"dir/deeper/foo.jar")   // true
      Glob.parse(t"*.jar").matches(t"dir/foo.jar")             // false: `*` does not cross `/`
      t"h?llo*.jar".as[Glob]                  // Glob(Exact('h'), OneChar, Exact('l'), …)
      ```

      Parsing is total: every text is a glob, since any character outside the wildcard
      syntax is an `Exact` token.
  refer kaleidoscope.g
  refer kaleidoscope.Regex
  entry .parse   reads a glob from text; never fails
      `*` is `Star`, and a second `*` directly after it makes the pair a `Globstar`; `?` is
      `OneChar`; `[…]` is a `Range` for the three-character `a-z` form or a `Specific` set
      otherwise, each `inverse` when its content begins with `!`; anything else is `Exact`.
  entry .decodable   reads a glob with `as[Glob]`: the `Decodable in Text` given, in implicit scope
      `Glob.parse`, as a codec, so a glob arrives from a command-line flag or a
      configuration file like any other decodable value, with no error to handle.
  entry .Token   one element of a glob: a wildcard, a character class, or a literal character
    detail
        `Star` is `*`, any run of characters within a segment; `Globstar` is `**`, any run
        across segments; `OneChar` is `?`; `Exact(char)` is a literal character; `Range(start,
        end, inverse)` is `[a-z]` or `[!a-z]`; and `Specific(chars, inverse)` is `[aeiou]` or
        `[!aeiou]`. `#regex` is each token's translation.
    entry #regex   the token as regular-expression text, with a literal escaped where it must be
        `Star` is `[^/\\]*`, `Globstar` is `.*`, `OneChar` is `[^/\\]`, a range is `[a-z]` or
        `[^a-z]`, a set is `[aeiou]` or `[^aeiou]`, and an `Exact` character is itself,
        preceded by `\` if it is one of the regular-expression metacharacters.
  entry #tokens   the glob's tokens, in order
  entry #regex   the glob translated to a regular expression, as `Text`
  entry #matches   whether the whole text matches the glob, on the engine `regexBackends` selects
      Takes a `RegexBackend[form]` (defaulting to `regexBackends.javaBaseRegex`, so an
      `import regexBackends.re2` switches it, as it does the interpolators) and that form's
      `Regex.Engine`. The engines cache compilation by rendered pattern, so matching many
      candidates against one glob compiles it once.

entry .internal   the macros behind `r"…"` and `g"…"`, and the extractor classes they expand to
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object houses the interpolators' expansion and the two classes a
      pattern expands into. `expandRegexJvm`, `expandRegexRe2`, `expandGlobJvm` and
      `expandGlobRe2` are the transparent inline entry points `r` and `g` dispatch to, by
      whether a `RegexBackend[Re2]` is in scope; `regexJvm`, `regexRe2`, `globJvm` and
      `globRe2` are their macro implementations. Each parses the parts with `Regex.parse`,
      reporting a `Regex.Error` as a compile error positioned at the offending character,
      computes the capture types from the groups' quantifiers, and splices a `NoExtraction` or
      an `RExtractor`; under RE2 it also parses the pattern into a praxinoscope `Motif`,
      rejecting anything outside the RE2 subset, and stages the motif — and for a
      captureless pattern a static `Fsa` matcher — into the extractor.
  entry .expandRegexJvm   expands `r"…"` for the `java.util.regex` engine
  entry .expandRegexRe2   expands `r"…"` for the RE2 engine
  entry .expandGlobJvm   expands `g"…"` for the `java.util.regex` engine
  entry .expandGlobRe2   expands `g"…"` for the RE2 engine
  entry .regexJvm   the macro behind `expandRegexJvm`
  entry .regexRe2   the macro behind `expandRegexRe2`
  entry .globJvm   the macro behind `expandGlobJvm`: translates each part with `Glob.parse` first
  entry .globRe2   the macro behind `expandGlobRe2`
  entry .NoExtraction   the extractor a pattern with no captures expands to
    detail
        Holds the pattern text, the staged `Motif` (installed into the RE2 engine's cache when
        the extractor is constructed) and, when one could be generated, the static `Fsa`
        which answers a whole-input match without the engine.
    entry #apply   the pattern as a `Regex in form`, for a literal in expression position
    entry #unapply   whether the text matches: by the `Fsa` when there is one and no scanner position is set
  entry .RExtractor   the extractor a pattern with captures expands to, typed by its captures
    detail
        `RExtractor[result, form]` holds the pattern's parts and the staged `Motif`;
        `result` is `Option[T]` for one capture of type `T`, or `Option[(T1, …, Tn)]` for
        several.
    entry #unapply   the captures of a match as the typed result, or `None`

